var WK_JAPAN = [
{"id":"japan-staple-dashi","name":"Dashi","nameAlt":"Ichiban Dashi — the first stock","aliases":["Dashi","Ichiban Dashi","Japanese stock","Kombu and Bonito Stock"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","basic","soup"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","batch","quick"],"ingredients":"300ml cold water · 3g dried kombu (a piece about 8cm square) · 5g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","method":"Almost everything Japanese food tastes of starts here, and the entire technique is a lesson in restraint: two ingredients, no browning, no salt, no boiling, and it is finished in under fifteen minutes. Understand it once and half of Japanese cooking opens up. Start with the kombu, and start it cold. Do not scrub the fine white bloom off the surface — that powder is mannitol and glutamate drying out of the seaweed itself, which is to say it is the flavour you are paying for, and every recipe that tells you to wipe the kombu clean is telling you to wash off the point. If there is visible grit or sand, brush it off dry with a cloth, and leave the white alone. Put the kombu in the cold water and give it time. Thirty minutes is good, a few hours in the fridge is better, and overnight is better still — a cold soak extracts the glutamate slowly and cleanly and asks nothing of you. Now heat it, and heat it gently, and here is the one rule of dashi: THE KOMBU MUST NEVER BOIL. Bring the pan up slowly over a low flame, aiming to take a good ten minutes to reach the point where small bubbles are beginning to gather on the bottom of the pan and the water is steaming but not moving — around 60 to 65°C, which in practical terms is the moment before it looks like anything is happening. Pull the kombu out at that point with tongs. Past roughly 80°C the seaweed starts giving up alginates instead of glutamate, and the stock turns slippery, faintly bitter and unmistakably marine in a way it should not be. That change is not reversible, and a boiled dashi cannot be corrected by any later step. Once the kombu is out, bring the water to a proper boil for a few seconds, then kill the heat completely and let it settle for thirty seconds. Now add all the katsuobushi at once and DO NOT STIR IT. Stirring beats the flakes apart, clouds the stock and pushes bitterness out of them; left alone they will swell, sink and release what they have in about a minute. Give them sixty to ninety seconds, no more, then strain the whole thing through a fine sieve lined with a piece of muslin or a clean cloth. And then the second rule, which matters as much as the first: DO NOT SQUEEZE THE FLAKES. Let it drip, and let it take a minute or two. Squeezing the sodden bonito is exactly the same mistake as boiling the kombu and produces the same result — a cloudy stock with a bitter, slightly fishy edge — and it is the most common way a home cook ruins a dashi they otherwise made perfectly. What you should have is a clear, pale golden liquid that smells of the sea and of woodsmoke and tastes savoury, round and almost sweet, with no salt in it at all. Taste it on its own before you use it, because you need to know what it tastes like unseasoned in order to recognise it later underneath everything else. Do not throw the spent kombu and katsuobushi away. They still hold a great deal, and the second extraction is a real ingredient in its own right — see the leftovers below.","cookTime":"Soak 30 min (or overnight) · Heat 10 min · Steep 2 min","kcal":"~5–10 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 1g, Carbs 0g, Fat 0g, Sodium ~60mg (before any seasoning)","storage":"Best on the day it is made, and its aroma is at its peak in the first hour. It keeps 3 days in a sealed jar in the fridge and freezes beautifully — an ice-cube tray of dashi is one of the most useful things you can have in a freezer, because it turns any pan of vegetables into a Japanese dish in a minute. Freeze it flat in a bag if you would rather have sheets you can snap.","chefNotes":"Kombu into cold water and never let it boil — pull it at 60–65°C. Katsuobushi off the heat, no stirring, 60–90 seconds. Strain and never squeeze. If your dashi is cloudy or bitter, one of those three is why.","pairsWith":"It is the base rather than the partner — miso soup, chawanmushi, every simmered vegetable, the batter of an okonomiyaki and the broth of a donburi. Keep a jar in the fridge and you will find uses for it that have nothing to do with Japan.","trivia":"Umami was discovered in this exact bowl. In 1908 Kikunae Ikeda, a chemistry professor at Tokyo Imperial University, became preoccupied with a question about his wife's dashi: it tasted strongly of something that was clearly not sweet, sour, salty or bitter, and the four-taste model of the day had no room for it. He boiled down a very large quantity of kombu, crystallised out the compound responsible, identified it as glutamic acid, and named the taste umami — savoury. He patented the manufacture of its sodium salt the following year, which is how monosodium glutamate came to exist. The better half of the story arrived five years later: in 1913 his student Shintaro Kodama went looking for the same thing in katsuobushi and found something different, inosinate, the savoury compound of dried fish and meat. Glutamate and inosinate are synergistic, and not mildly so — put together they register as several times more savoury than the sum of the two alone. So the two-ingredient stock that Japanese cooks had been making for centuries turns out to be a precisely built chemical amplifier, and it took Western analytical chemistry until the twentieth century to explain what the technique had already worked out. The katsuobushi deserves a word of its own, because it may be the most laboriously made ingredient in any kitchen: bonito is filleted, simmered, boned, smoked over weeks, then deliberately inoculated with a mould, sun-dried, re-inoculated, and repeated over months until the fillet is dry, dense and hard enough to be shaved on what is essentially a woodworking plane. It is routinely described as the hardest food in the world, and that is not a figure of speech.","howThisFeels":"That first smell as the flakes go in and the steam comes up — smoke and clean sea and something warm underneath it — and the small surprise of tasting a liquid with no salt in it that is somehow already delicious.","leftovers":["(easy) The spent kombu and katsuobushi make niban dashi, the second stock, and this is standard practice rather than thrift: cover them with the same volume of fresh water, simmer gently for 10 minutes — this time boiling does no harm because the delicate compounds are already gone — add a small pinch of fresh flakes at the end, and strain. It is rougher and less fragrant than the first, and it is what miso soup and long-simmered vegetables actually want.","(easy) After the second extraction, chop the soft kombu finely and simmer it with soy, mirin and a little sugar until sticky and dark. This is tsukudani, it keeps a fortnight in the fridge, and a teaspoon on hot rice is a complete small pleasure.","(creative) Dry the spent bonito flakes in a low oven, then blitz them with sesame seeds, a little salt and a torn sheet of nori into a coarse powder. That is a homemade furikake, it costs you nothing, and it will improve rice, eggs, popcorn and roast vegetables for weeks."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Kombu & Shiitake (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":4,"howThisFeels":"Deeper and darker than the classic, and it smells of a forest floor rather than the sea — the version that makes vegetables taste like they have meat behind them.","trivia":"This is shojin dashi, the stock of Japanese Buddhist temple cooking, and it is not a substitute so much as the older sibling. Temple kitchens have worked without fish for centuries, and the dried shiitake is doing the same job the bonito does in the classic: mushrooms concentrate guanylate as they dry, which is a third savoury compound, and it is synergistic with the kombu's glutamate in exactly the same way inosinate is. A dried shiitake gives up far more to its soaking water than a fresh one ever gives a pan, which is why the soaking liquid here is not a by-product but half the recipe.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"5g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","to":"8g dried shiitake mushrooms"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Soak the dried shiitake in the same cold water as the kombu from the start — they need hours, not minutes, and cold water is what gets the flavour out without turning the mushrooms to mush. Overnight in the fridge is ideal. Lift both the kombu and the mushrooms out before the water reaches 65°C, then bring the liquid to a bare simmer for 5 minutes and take it off. Strain it, and POUR OFF THE LAST CENTIMETRE rather than tipping the whole pan through the sieve, because grit settles out of dried mushrooms and it will follow the liquid if you let it. The soaked mushrooms themselves are now a good ingredient with the caps sliced into a stir-fry or a rice bowl; do not bin them."}]}},{"name":"Classic Ichiban Dashi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":9},{"name":"Niboshi (Dried Sardine) Dashi","icon":"🐟","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":7,"howThisFeels":"Assertive, oily and properly fishy in the best way — the stock that stands up to a heavy miso and does not disappear underneath it.","trivia":"The western half of Japan tends to reach for niboshi rather than katsuobushi, and it makes a noticeably more aggressive stock — this is the base under a lot of miso soup and a great deal of ramen, and it is considerably cheaper than bonito. It also has a preparation step that is not optional and is nearly always skipped: the heads and the black gut of each little sardine must be pinched out before soaking. Those are where the bitterness lives, and a niboshi dashi made from whole fish tastes harsh in a way people wrongly blame on the sardines themselves. The best method is not to heat it at all — a cold overnight steep in the fridge gives a cleaner, rounder stock than any amount of careful simmering.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"5g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","to":"8g niboshi (dried sardines), heads and guts removed"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Pinch the head off each sardine and press out the dark line of gut with a thumbnail — it takes two minutes for a handful and it is the difference between a good stock and a bitter one. Add them to the cold water alongside the kombu and leave the whole thing in the fridge overnight, then simply strain it: no heat at all. If you want it the same day, heat it slowly with the kombu as written, pull the kombu at 65°C, then hold the sardines at a bare simmer for 8 minutes and strain without pressing."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"japan-shoyu-ramen","name":"Shoyu Ramen","nameAlt":"Soy-seasoned ramen, Tokyo style","aliases":["Shoyu Ramen","Soy Sauce Ramen","Tokyo Ramen","Ramen"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","soup"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["weekend","project","family","winter"],"ingredients":"300g chicken wings and carcass, chopped · 60g pork belly, in one piece, for the chashu · 1.2L cold water · 8g dried kombu · 6g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes) · 25g onion, halved · 15g fresh ginger, sliced · 10g garlic, crushed · 3 spring onions · 45ml light soy sauce (for the tare) · 15ml mirin · 15ml sake · 8g white sugar · 120g fresh ramen noodles · 1 egg · 20ml neutral oil (for the aroma oil) · 15g spring onion greens (for the aroma oil) · 20g menma (seasoned bamboo shoots), optional · 1/4 sheet nori","method":"The single most useful thing to understand about ramen is that a bowl is not one recipe but THREE separate things made separately and assembled at the last second: the soup, the tare, and the aroma oil. And the second most useful thing follows from it, and it is the thing almost every home cook gets wrong: THE BOWL IS SEASONED BY THE TARE, NOT BY THE SOUP. The broth itself is left essentially unsalted. If you taste your finished ramen broth on its own and it tastes properly seasoned and delicious, you have made a soup, and when the tare and the noodles go in you will get something too salty and flat. It should taste rich, savoury and slightly underwhelming on its own. That is correct. Start the soup. Cover the chopped chicken bones with cold water in a pot, bring it to the boil, and let it boil hard for two or three minutes, at which point a great deal of grey scum will rise. Tip the whole lot into the sink, then wash each piece under the tap and SCRUB the bones with your fingers, and wash the pot as well. This is fifteen minutes of unglamorous work and it is what separates a clear golden broth from a murky beige one. Return the bones to the clean pot with fresh cold water, the onion, ginger, garlic and the white parts of the spring onion, bring it barely to the boil and then drop it immediately to the faintest possible movement — an occasional bubble, nothing more. Three hours at that whisper. Add the kombu for the last thirty minutes only and never let it boil, and the katsuobushi for the last two minutes off the heat, exactly as in the dashi. Strain. Meanwhile, roll the pork belly tightly and tie it, brown it all over in a dry pan, then simmer it very gently for two hours in a small pan of water with a splash of soy, mirin and sake until a skewer meets no resistance. Cool it in its liquid and chill it before slicing; a warm belly cannot be cut thin and thin is the whole point of chashu. Now the tare, which takes four minutes and does most of the work. Warm the soy, mirin, sake and sugar together with a small piece of kombu until the sugar dissolves, and do not boil it — you want the mirin and sake to lose their raw alcohol edge, not to reduce. Take it off, let the kombu sit in it while it cools, and remove it. That is roughly enough tare for two or three bowls, and it will keep in the fridge for weeks. The aroma oil is faster still: warm the neutral oil with the sliced spring onion greens over a low heat for six or seven minutes until the greens are frizzled and the oil smells strongly of them, then strain. Do it while something else is simmering. The egg wants six and a half minutes from the fridge into already-boiling water, then straight into iced water, then peeled and left in a splash of the tare thinned with water for four hours or overnight. Do not leave it in the marinade longer than about twelve hours; past that the white turns rubbery and unpleasantly salty, and there is no way back from it. Now assemble, and assemble fast, because this is where a good bowl is won or lost. Fill the serving bowl with boiling water to heat it through, and heat the soup to a genuine rolling boil at the same time. Put a big pot of UNSALTED water on for the noodles — unsalted because everything about this bowl has been measured, and salted noodle water quietly adds an amount of salt nobody accounted for. Tip out the hot water from the bowl. Into the empty hot bowl goes 30ml of tare and a teaspoon of the aroma oil. Pour about 300ml of boiling soup in on top of them and swirl it once; the pouring does the mixing. Cook the noodles in the meantime — fresh ramen noodles want 60 to 90 seconds and no more — then lift them out and SHAKE THEM BONE DRY, hard, several times. Water clinging to noodles is the last enemy: it dilutes a tare you measured to the millilitre and turns a sharp bowl into a vague one. Fold the noodles neatly into the soup, top with sliced chashu, the halved egg, menma, sliced spring onion and the nori tucked against the rim, and take it to the table immediately. From the moment the noodles hit the water you have about three minutes before the bowl starts declining, which is why every ramen shop in Japan is built around a counter.","cookTime":"Broth 3 hr · Chashu 2 hr · Tare 5 min · Assembly 3 min","kcal":"~640–780 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g, Carbs 72g, Fat 26g, Sodium ~2100mg","storage":"Never store an assembled bowl — noodles sitting in hot broth are ruined within about ten minutes and there is no rescuing them. Store the components instead, which is how shops do it: broth keeps 4 days in the fridge and freezes for 3 months, tare keeps a month in a jar, aroma oil keeps 2 weeks, chashu keeps 4 days in its liquid and slices better on day two than on day one. With those in the fridge a bowl is a five-minute job on a weeknight, which is the real reason to make the full version on a Sunday.","chefNotes":"Three components, not one recipe. The tare seasons the bowl, so the broth stays almost unsalted. Blanch and scrub the bones. Unsalted noodle water, and shake the noodles bone dry. Assemble in twenty seconds and eat immediately.","pairsWith":"Gyoza on the side is the standard pairing and needs no defending. A cold beer, a small dish of something pickled and sharp, and nothing else — ramen is not a dish that wants company.","trivia":"Japan's most famous food is Chinese, and Japan said so in the name for the first half of the twentieth century: until the 1950s it was generally called shina soba or chuka soba, both of which simply mean Chinese noodles. It arrived with Chinese cooks in the treaty ports after 1859, and Yokohama's Chinatown is the usual starting point. What turned a port-city curiosity into the national dish, though, was neither Chinese nor Japanese but American. Japan's rice harvest failed catastrophically in 1945 and the country was starving; the occupation authorities shipped in enormous quantities of surplus American wheat flour as food aid, and the Japanese government, holding a mountain of wheat and almost no rice, actively promoted bread and noodles in schools and public campaigns. Cheap flour plus a hungry population plus a black market full of small stalls is the entire recipe for ramen's explosion, and nearly every regional style that people now discuss as though it were ancient tradition was invented in the decades immediately after: Sapporo's miso ramen in the 1950s, Hakata's tonkotsu around the same time, the instant packet in 1958. The dish is younger than most of the people who tell you how it should be made, which is worth remembering when someone gets very serious about the rules.","howThisFeels":"The moment the boiling broth hits the tare in the bottom of the bowl and the whole kitchen suddenly smells like a shop on a cold night — and then that first pull of noodles, too hot, eaten anyway.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover broth is the single most valuable thing in the fridge — use it instead of water to cook rice, or heat it with a nest of noodles and whatever greens are wilting for a five-minute lunch that tastes like it took three hours, because it did.","(easy) Leftover chashu is superb cold: slice it thin, torch or grill it briefly until the edges blister, and eat it on rice with a spoonful of tare and a raw egg yolk. This is a real dish in Japan and not a scrap-eating exercise.","(creative) Chop leftover chashu small and fry it hard with cold rice, the last of the aroma oil and a beaten egg, finishing with a spoon of tare instead of soy sauce. It is the best fried rice you will make, and every element of it was already seasoned for you."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Shiitake & Kombu Shoyu (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Dark, woody and startlingly deep for a bowl with nothing in it — the version that convinces people the meat was never the point.","trivia":"A vegan ramen has one real problem and it is not flavour but body: meat broth is full of gelatine, and gelatine is what makes a spoonful of soup cling to the noodles instead of running off them. Flavour is easy to replace with dried mushrooms and kombu; that clinging weight is not. Two things do most of the work. A small handful of soaked and blitzed cashews or a spoonful of white sesame paste emulsified into the hot broth adds fat and viscosity at once, and roasting the mushrooms and onion hard before they go in the pot builds the browned depth that a long bone simmer would otherwise provide. Skip both and you get a pleasant mushroom tea; do both and you get ramen.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"300g chicken wings and carcass, chopped"},{"item":"60g pork belly, in one piece, for the chashu"},{"item":"6g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"25g dried shiitake mushrooms"},{"item":"150g fresh mushrooms, halved"},{"item":"15g white sesame paste or 20g cashews"},{"item":"80g firm tofu, pressed and cubed"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Roast the fresh mushrooms and the halved onion at 220°C for 25 minutes until they are properly browned at the edges — pale roasted vegetables give a pale broth. Soak the dried shiitake in the cold water for several hours, then build the broth from the soaking liquid, the roasted vegetables, the ginger, garlic and spring onion whites, simmered gently for 45 minutes; add the kombu for the last 20 and never boil it. Strain, POURING OFF THE LAST CENTIMETRE because mushroom grit settles. Whisk the sesame paste or the blitzed cashews into the hot strained broth until it is smooth and no longer separating — this is what gives it body, and skipping it is the difference between ramen and soup. Fry the pressed tofu cubes hard on all sides until they have a real skin, and use them in place of the chashu. The tare is unchanged and already vegan."}]}},{"name":"Classic Tokyo Shoyu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48},{"name":"The Full Bowl — Rolled Chashu, Ajitama & Menma","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":62,"howThisFeels":"Two slabs of belly with a lacquered edge, a yolk that is still liquid at the centre, and the sense that you have built something rather than cooked it.","trivia":"The full bowl is where ramen stops being a recipe and becomes a project, and the component worth the most attention is the egg. An ajitama is a soft-boiled egg with a jammy — not runny, not set — yolk, and the timing is unforgiving because it is measured from a fridge-cold egg into water that is already at a rolling boil: six and a half minutes, and thirty seconds either side is a visibly different egg. The ice bath is not optional either, because carryover heat will keep cooking the yolk for another minute if you let it, and by then the window has closed. The last refinement is the finishing torch on the chashu: a blowtorch or a very hot grill for thirty seconds renders the fat cap and blisters the edge, and it is the one restaurant trick that transfers to a home kitchen without any equipment worth arguing about.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"60g pork belly, in one piece, for the chashu","to":"140g pork belly, in one piece, rolled and tied for the chashu"}],"addIng":[{"item":"1 extra egg"},{"item":"10g garlic, extra, for the aroma oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Roll the belly skin-side out into a tight cylinder and tie it at 2cm intervals with butcher's string — the tying is what gives you round slices rather than ragged strips, and a loose roll unravels in the simmer. Brown it hard on every face in a dry pan before it goes into the braise, then simmer it two and a half hours and, crucially, LET IT COOL COMPLETELY IN ITS OWN LIQUID before it goes anywhere near a knife. Chill it overnight if you can. Slice it 5mm thick, lay the slices in the bowl on top of the noodles, and blister the top of each one with a blowtorch or under a fierce grill for thirty seconds before serving. Marinate two eggs rather than one and halve them with a wet knife, wiping the blade between cuts, or the yolk drags."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"japan-tonkatsu","name":"Tonkatsu","nameAlt":"Panko-crumbed pork cutlet","aliases":["Tonkatsu","Pork Katsu","Japanese Pork Cutlet","Katsu"],"course":"main","type":["main","roast"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","weekend"],"ingredients":"180g pork loin, cut 2.5cm thick, in one piece · 3g fine salt · 1g white pepper · 20g cake flour · 1 egg, beaten · 45g panko breadcrumbs · 500ml neutral oil, for frying · 120g white cabbage, very finely shredded · 30ml tonkatsu sauce · 5g Japanese mustard (karashi), optional · 1 lemon wedge","method":"A tonkatsu is a thick cutlet, and every rule in it exists because of that thickness. Thin it out and you have a schnitzel, which is a good thing but a different thing; the whole ambition here is a crust that shatters over meat that is still faintly pink and running with juice, and that is a heat-management problem before it is anything else. Start with the pork, and start with the step that is left out of most recipes and explains most failures. Along one edge of a loin chop there is a band of fat, and between that fat and the meat there is a thin white line of connective tissue. That tissue shrinks far harder and faster than muscle does, and if you leave it intact the cutlet will pull itself into a bowl shape in the oil, which means the middle lifts clear of the pan, cooks unevenly and comes out with a pale hinge across it. So take a small knife and SNIP THROUGH THAT LINE at three or four points along the edge, cutting about a centimetre in. It takes ten seconds and it is the single most useful thing in this method. Now season it: salt and white pepper on both faces, and then leave it fifteen minutes at room temperature. The salt draws a little moisture out and then carries itself back in, seasoning the meat rather than just its surface, and the rest takes the fridge-chill off so the centre is not starting from cold. Before crumbing, pat both faces completely dry with kitchen paper. Set up three shallow dishes: flour, beaten egg, panko. The flour is a DUSTING and nothing more — press the cutlet in, then hold it up and tap it firmly so that almost all of it falls away, leaving a barely visible film. A thick coat of flour hydrates into a paste under the egg, and that paste is what makes a crust that slides off the meat in one piece when you cut it. Through the egg, letting the excess run off, and then into the panko: press it on firmly on both faces and around the edges, then lift it, shake off what is loose, and DO NOT PRESS IT AGAIN. Compacting panko defeats its entire purpose. And now the frying, which is done at two temperatures, and the reason is thickness rather than tradition. A 2.5cm cutlet fried at one temperature gives you a choice between raw in the middle and burnt on the outside; there is no single number that does both. So: heat the oil to 160°C, lower the cutlet in away from you, and fry it for six or seven minutes, turning once, until it is a pale even gold. It will not look finished, and it should not. Lift it onto a rack — a rack, not paper, so steam escapes from underneath instead of soaking the crust you have just built — and STAND IT UP ON ITS EDGE if it will balance, so both faces drain. Rest it there for four minutes. That rest is not a pause; carryover heat is finishing the centre while the crust dries. Now bring the oil up to 190°C and put the cutlet back in for thirty to sixty seconds, purely for colour and crispness. Out onto the rack again for two minutes. While all this is happening, deal with the cabbage, which is not a garnish and should not be treated as one. Shred it as finely as you physically can — fine enough to be almost translucent — then drop it into a bowl of iced water for ten minutes and spin or shake it thoroughly dry. Limp cabbage becomes crisp, sweet and cold, and a cold sweet crunch is the exact thing a plate of fried pork needs. Slice the tonkatsu across into 2cm strips with the sharpest knife you own, using one long draw rather than a sawing motion; sawing tears the crust off the meat. Fan the slices against a heap of cabbage, put the sauce in a small dish rather than over the top, and add the lemon and a small dab of karashi if you have it. Rice, and a bowl of miso soup, and it is done.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · First fry 7 min · Rest 4 min · Second fry 1 min","kcal":"~680–820 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 42g, Carbs 44g, Fat 40g, Sodium ~980mg","storage":"Eat it inside ten minutes; a rested tonkatsu is a good sandwich but it is no longer a tonkatsu. It will keep 2 days in the fridge and reheats better than most fried food if you use a hot oven or an air fryer at 200°C for 6–7 minutes on a rack — never a microwave, which turns panko to wet sand in about forty seconds. The frying oil, cooled and strained through a cloth, is good for several more uses.","chefNotes":"Snip the membrane along the fat edge or it curls. Dust the flour and tap almost all of it off. Press the panko once and never again. Two temperatures — 160°C to cook, rest, then 190°C for colour. Ice-water the shredded cabbage.","pairsWith":"Rice and miso soup, and the shredded cabbage that comes with it as a matter of law. A sharp pickle on the side, and nothing rich — the plate is already doing that.","trivia":"Tonkatsu is yoshoku — Western food, made Japanese — and it is the clearest example of the category. Its ancestor is the French cotelette, and it exists at all because of a deliberate act of government policy. For roughly twelve centuries Japan had an effective prohibition on eating four-legged animals, dating from an imperial edict of 675 AD and renewed often enough to hold; meat-eating was Buddhist taboo and socially unthinkable for most people. Then in 1872 the Meiji Emperor publicly ate beef, and it was reported as news, because the entire point was to announce that the prohibition was over and that Japan intended to modernise, diet included. It was not universally welcomed — a group of men broke into the Imperial Palace in protest at the change and several were shot. Western restaurants followed, and in 1899 Rengatei in Ginza took the pan-fried veal cutlet and made three changes to it: pork instead of veal, deep-fried in a deep vat like tempura rather than pan-fried in butter, and — the decisive one — served already sliced into strips, with rice and chopsticks and a heap of raw shredded cabbage, instead of whole with a knife and fork. Those three changes are the entire distance between a French dish and a Japanese one. The cabbage, by most accounts, was not a culinary decision at all: the restaurant was short-staffed during the war with China and raw shredded cabbage was simply faster to produce than cooked vegetables. It turned out to be the right answer, and it never left.","howThisFeels":"The specific noise a good tonkatsu makes under the knife — a dry crackle, quite loud — and the pale pink centre steaming as the slices fall apart against the cold cabbage.","leftovers":["(easy) A katsu sando is a real and famous thing and not a consolation prize: cold or briefly reheated tonkatsu, a smear of sauce, a little mustard, between two slices of soft white bread with the crusts cut off. Press it gently, cut it in half, and it is arguably better than the original.","(easy) Slice leftover cutlet thin and lay it over a bowl of hot rice with a spoonful of the sauce and a soft egg — that is most of the way to katsudon and takes four minutes.","(creative) Chop cold tonkatsu into cubes, crust and all, and fold it through fried rice at the very last second so the panko keeps some texture. The crumb turns into little crisp nuggets through the rice and the whole thing tastes deliberate rather than salvaged."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Chicken Katsu (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"Softer and juicier than the pork, with the crust doing even more of the work — the version children ask for by name.","trivia":"Chicken katsu is not a lesser tonkatsu, it is a different technical problem, and the difference is thickness again. A chicken thigh is thin and uneven, so the two-temperature method that the pork needs is wasted on it — there is no thick centre to worry about. Fry it once, at a steady 175°C, for about five minutes. Use thigh rather than breast: breast is lean enough that by the time the crust is properly golden the meat has gone dry and stringy, whereas thigh has enough fat to stay juicy through the same fry, and it also folds to an even thickness in a way breast does not. Open the thigh out flat, butterfly the thick part, and press it into a rough rectangle before crumbing.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g pork loin, cut 2.5cm thick, in one piece","to":"180g chicken thighs, boned and opened out flat"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Open each boned thigh out skin-side down, slash the thickest part and fold it back so the piece is roughly even, then flatten it lightly with the heel of your hand — not with a mallet, which tears it. There is no membrane to snip on a thigh, so that step falls away. Season, rest 15 minutes, pat dry and crumb exactly as written. Fry ONCE at a steady 175°C for about 5 minutes, turning halfway, until deep gold; there is no thick centre here so the two-stage fry gains you nothing and only risks drying it out. Rest on a rack for 2 minutes and slice."}]}},{"name":"Classic Tonkatsu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42},{"name":"Katsu Curry","icon":"🍛","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"Dark, thick, faintly sweet curry poured beside — never over — the crust, so you get one bite crisp, the next bite drowned, and both on purpose.","trivia":"Japanese curry arrived from Britain rather than from India, which is why it is thick, mild and brown rather than thin and hot: it came in with the Royal Navy in the Meiji period as a British Indian-style stew, was adopted by the Imperial Japanese Navy as a Friday ration because it was cheap, filling and easy to cook at sea, and spread through the country as sailors went home. It is thickened with a roux, which is a European technique and not an Indian one, and that roux is the whole texture of the dish. Two details make a homemade one taste like a shop one. First, cook the onions far longer than feels sensible — thirty to forty minutes until they are genuinely brown and jammy — because that sweetness is the base note under the spice. Second, a grated apple and a spoonful of honey are standard rather than eccentric, and they are what give Japanese curry its characteristic rounded sweetness. Serve the sauce beside the cutlet and never poured over it, so the crust survives to the last slice.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g onion, thinly sliced"},{"item":"60g carrot, cut into chunks"},{"item":"80g potato, cut into chunks"},{"item":"20g butter"},{"item":"18g cake flour, extra, for the roux"},{"item":"8g mild curry powder"},{"item":"3g garam masala"},{"item":"40g apple, grated"},{"item":"10ml honey"},{"item":"300ml chicken or vegetable stock"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the curry first — it is happy to sit while you fry. Cook the sliced onion in the butter over a low heat for a full 30 to 40 minutes, stirring now and then, until it is deep brown, sweet and collapsed. Rushing this is the single reason a home Japanese curry tastes thin. Push the onion aside, stir the extra flour into the butter and cook it for 2 minutes until it smells biscuity, then stir in the curry powder and garam masala and give them 30 seconds in the fat — dry spice bloomed in fat tastes of something, dry spice dropped into liquid does not. Add the stock a little at a time, whisking each addition smooth before the next, or you will get lumps that never come out. Add the carrot, potato, grated apple and honey, and simmer 25 minutes until the potato is soft and the sauce coats a spoon thickly. Fry and slice the tonkatsu exactly as written, put the rice on one side of the plate and the sliced cutlet next to it, and pour the curry over the RICE and against the edge of the cutlet — never over the crust."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-okonomiyaki","name":"Okonomiyaki","nameAlt":"Osaka cabbage pancake","aliases":["Okonomiyaki","Japanese Savoury Pancake","Osaka Pancake","Cabbage Pancake"],"course":"main","type":["main","street-food","bread"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","street-food","quick"],"ingredients":"180g white cabbage, cut into 1cm squares · 55g cake flour · 60ml dashi, cold · 1 egg · 35g nagaimo (mountain yam), grated — or 2g baking powder as the stand-in · 2 spring onions, sliced · 20g pickled red ginger (beni shoga), chopped · 60g pork belly, sliced very thin · 15ml neutral oil · 30ml okonomiyaki sauce · 20ml Japanese-style mayonnaise · 2g aonori (dried green seaweed flakes) · 4g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","method":"The name means as-you-like-it, grilled, and the name is a licence — but there are four things that are not up to you, and they are the difference between the pancake people rave about and the heavy grey disc most people produce at home. Understand first what this actually is. It is NOT a pancake with cabbage in it. It is a large quantity of cabbage held together by a very small quantity of batter, and the ratio is roughly equal weights of cabbage to everything else combined. If your mixture looks like batter with some cabbage stirred through, you have too much batter, and the result will be dense and doughy. It should look, worryingly, like a bowl of cabbage that will never hold together. Cut the cabbage rather than grating or shredding it: rough 1cm squares. Grated cabbage bleeds water and collapses, and you want distinct pieces that steam inside and keep a little bite. Make the batter first and separately: whisk the flour, cold dashi and egg together until just smooth, then grate the nagaimo in. Nagaimo is a mountain yam and when grated it goes slimy and stringy in a way that looks alarming and is exactly right — it aerates the batter and makes the finished pancake light, fluffy and slightly custardy inside rather than flat and chewy. It is genuinely hard to find outside a well-stocked Asian grocer, and rather than pretend otherwise: the honest stand-in is 2g of baking powder and a slightly wetter batter, which gets you most of the lift, though not the texture. Let the batter rest twenty minutes in the fridge. Now the third rule: FOLD THE CABBAGE IN AT THE LAST SECOND, immediately before it goes in the pan, and never before. Cabbage and salt and time make water, and a mixture that has been standing for ten minutes is a wet mixture that will not brown. Fold the cabbage, spring onion and pickled ginger through the rested batter with a spatula, using cutting strokes from the bottom, ten or twelve turns at most. Do not beat it, do not whisk it, do not try to make it smooth. You are coating cabbage, not making a batter, and every extra stir develops gluten and adds weight. Get a heavy pan or a flat griddle to a steady medium heat with the oil — medium, not high, because this needs eight or nine minutes and a hot pan burns the outside long before the middle sets. Pile the mixture in and shape it gently into a round about 2cm thick and 15cm across, using the spatula to tuck the edge in rather than to flatten the top. Lay the thin pork belly slices flat over the top, raw. And now the fourth rule, which is the one everybody breaks: DO NOT PRESS IT DOWN. Every instinct you have will tell you to squash it with the spatula, and it is the single most destructive thing you can do. Everything so far — the nagaimo, the loose folding, the cut cabbage — has been about getting air into it, and one press with a spatula puts you back to a flat rubbery disc with a wet middle. Leave it alone. Four to five minutes, until the edge looks set and dry and a spatula slides under cleanly. Then flip it once, confidently, in a single movement — use two spatulas if you want, and commit, because a hesitant flip is what breaks it. The pork is now against the pan and will crisp there. Another four or five minutes, then flip it back so the pork side is up for a final minute. It is cooked when a skewer into the centre comes out clean and hot. Onto the plate, and dress it in order: brush the okonomiyaki sauce over the top, zigzag the mayonnaise across it, drag a skewer through the lines if you want the marbled look, then scatter the aonori and finally a generous handful of katsuobushi. Take it to the table straight away, and watch the flakes.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Batter rest 20 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~520–620 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g, Carbs 48g, Fat 28g, Sodium ~1150mg","storage":"Best within minutes, and the toppings should never be applied to something you intend to keep — sauce and mayonnaise go soggy fast. An undressed pancake keeps 2 days in the fridge and reheats surprisingly well in a dry pan over medium heat for 3 minutes a side, which crisps the outside again; dress it after reheating, never before. It freezes acceptably undressed, wrapped tightly, for a month.","chefNotes":"It is cabbage held together by batter, not the other way round. Cut the cabbage into squares, fold it in at the last second, mix ten turns and no more — and never, ever press it down with the spatula.","pairsWith":"A cold beer, and a bowl of rice if you are treating it as dinner rather than as a snack. Miso soup alongside is standard at home. Nothing rich — the mayonnaise has that covered.","trivia":"There are very few famous dishes where the cooking surface is also the dining table, and okonomiyaki is the clearest one: in a great many restaurants a hot steel teppan is set into the table itself, the mixture arrives in a bowl, and the customer cooks their own. That single fact quietly explains most of the recipe. Everything in it has to survive an amateur — which is why nothing in the dish is delicate, why the cabbage is cut into robust squares rather than shredded fine, why the cooking temperature is a forgiving medium rather than a fierce heat, and why the sauce and mayonnaise are powerful enough to carry a pancake that has been flipped badly. It is a dish engineered around the certainty that the cook does not know what they are doing, and it is generous about it. The regional argument is real and institutional rather than a matter of taste: Hiroshima layers its version instead of mixing it, building crepe, cabbage, pork and a nest of fried noodles in strata, and the city contains a building of several floors housing nothing but okonomiyaki stalls, one after another, in competition. Hiroshima people call the Osaka method the mixed style; Osaka people simply call it okonomiyaki. And the katsuobushi on top is a small piece of deliberate theatre — the flakes are shaved so thin, a few microns, that the convection rising off the hot pancake lifts and moves them, so the whole surface appears to writhe. It settles as the plate cools, and it is meant to be seen.","howThisFeels":"That first flip landing cleanly, the smell of cabbage and pork fat hitting hot steel — and then the bonito flakes on top moving as though the thing were breathing.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheat an undressed pancake in a dry pan for 3 minutes a side and dress it fresh; it comes back far better than it has any right to, because the cabbage inside re-steams itself.","(easy) Leftover batter keeps a day in the fridge — fold fresh cabbage through it in the morning and make a smaller one for breakfast with an egg fried on top.","(creative) Cut a cold undressed pancake into fingers, crisp them hard in a dry pan and serve them with the sauce and mayonnaise as a dip. They eat like a savoury croquette and disappear faster than the original did."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Cabbage & Egg (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"Sweeter and cleaner without the pork fat, with the cabbage doing all the talking — the version that proves the pancake never needed the meat.","trivia":"Take the pork and the bonito flakes out and the dish barely notices, which tells you something about where its flavour actually comes from: the cabbage, the sauce and the browning. There is one thing to be careful about, and it is invisible. Both the dashi in the batter and the katsuobushi on top are fish, so a vegetarian version has to replace the stock as well as the topping — using the classic dashi and simply leaving off the flakes is the mistake people make. Use the kombu and shiitake dashi instead, which is a better match here anyway because its woodier depth stands in for the missing pork fat. In place of the bonito on top, a heavier scatter of aonori and some toasted sesame does the work.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g pork belly, sliced very thin"},{"item":"4g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"40g sweetcorn kernels"},{"item":"5g toasted sesame seeds"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use the kombu and shiitake dashi for the batter rather than the classic — the classic contains bonito and this version does not want it. Fold the sweetcorn in with the cabbage; it adds the sweetness and the little bursts of moisture that the pork fat was providing. With nothing laid on top, the first side needs a slightly longer 5 to 6 minutes so the surface sets properly before the flip. Finish with a heavier hand of aonori than usual and a scatter of toasted sesame in place of the bonito flakes. Check the okonomiyaki sauce you are using — several commercial ones contain fish extract, and a home mix of 3 parts tomato sauce, 2 parts Worcestershire-style sauce, 1 part soy and 1 part honey is close enough and takes a minute."}]}},{"name":"Classic Osaka Pork Belly","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26},{"name":"Hiroshima-Style, Layered with Noodles","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"A tower rather than a pancake — crisp noodles, a mountain of sweet steamed cabbage and a soft fried egg holding the whole thing down.","trivia":"Hiroshima's version is not a variation on the Osaka one so much as a rebuttal of it. Nothing is mixed: a paper-thin crepe goes down first, then an enormous heap of cabbage on top of it, then the pork, and the whole tower is flipped as a unit and left to steam under its own weight for several minutes while a nest of noodles fries alongside and an egg cooks flat on the griddle. It is then assembled in reverse. The engineering reason for the layers is that the cabbage steams rather than fries, so you can use far more of it — roughly double — without it going wet, and the sweetness of a large volume of gently steamed cabbage is the whole character of the dish. It is also considerably harder to do at home than the Osaka style, and the flip is the moment it lives or dies, which is precisely why Hiroshima cooks are proud of it.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100g cabbage, extra, shredded"},{"item":"120g cooked yakisoba or egg noodles"},{"item":"1 extra egg"},{"item":"30g beansprouts"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Do not mix anything. Thin the batter with an extra 30ml of dashi and spread a very thin crepe, about 18cm across, on the griddle. Dust it with a little aonori, then pile ALL the cabbage and the beansprouts on top in a tall heap — it will look absurd and it is meant to. Lay the thin pork slices over the heap. Cook 4 minutes, then slide two spatulas underneath and flip the entire tower in one committed movement; if it collapses at the edges, push it back in and carry on. Cover with a lid or a bowl and let it steam 6 to 8 minutes, during which the cabbage will sink to about a third of its height. Meanwhile fry the cooked noodles hard in a little oil on another part of the pan until the edges crisp, shape them into a round the same size as the pancake, and slide the whole tower on top of them. Fry the egg flat, slide the tower onto the egg, and flip the lot one final time so the egg is on top. Sauce, mayonnaise and aonori as usual."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-oyakodon","name":"Oyakodon","nameAlt":"Parent-and-child rice bowl","aliases":["Oyakodon","Chicken and Egg Rice Bowl","Oyako Donburi"],"course":"main","type":["main","breakfast"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","quick","family"],"ingredients":"120g chicken thighs, boned, cut into 2cm pieces · 60g onion, sliced thinly with the grain · 120ml dashi · 20ml light soy sauce · 20ml mirin · 10ml sake · 5g white sugar · 2 eggs · 200g hot cooked short-grain rice · 1 spring onion, sliced · 1g shichimi togarashi, to serve · 5g mitsuba or flat-leaf parsley, optional","method":"This is a ten-minute dish with one genuinely unusual instruction in it, and if you take nothing else from this card, take that one: it is served DELIBERATELY UNDERCOOKED, and the residual heat and the hot rice finish it in the bowl. A properly set oyakodon is an overcooked oyakodon. Everything else follows from that. First, the practical rule that shapes the whole method: THIS IS COOKED ONE PORTION AT A TIME AND IS NOT SCALED UP. In Japan it is made in a small dedicated pan roughly the width of the bowl it will slide into, and the reason is geometry rather than tradition. The dish depends on a shallow, wide layer of liquid so that the egg sets in seconds rather than minutes; double the quantity in the same pan and you have a deep pool that must be stirred, and stirring turns it into scrambled egg in broth. If you are feeding four people, cook four bowls one after another, and it will take you twelve minutes rather than three. Slice the onion with the grain rather than across it — with the grain the slices hold their shape and stay slightly sweet and firm; cut across, they collapse into strings. Put the dashi, soy, mirin, sake and sugar into a small frying pan or shallow saucepan, add the onion, and simmer it for three or four minutes until the onion is translucent and just tender. Taste the liquid at this point, because this is the only moment you can adjust anything: it should taste distinctly sweet-savoury and a little strong, since it is about to be diluted by two eggs and served over unseasoned rice. Add the chicken in a single layer, spread out, and simmer for four to five minutes until just cooked through. Do not stir it about; nudge the pieces once so they are evenly covered. Now the eggs, and here is the technique that makes the difference between this and a plain chicken-and-egg pan. Break the eggs into a bowl and beat them BADLY — five or six strokes with chopsticks or a fork, just enough to break the yolks and streak them through the white, and no further. You want visible ribbons of yolk and white rather than a uniform yellow, because those two set at different speeds and give the finished bowl its marbled, layered look. A properly beaten egg gives you a flat single-textured sheet. Then pour it in TWO POURS. Pour roughly two-thirds of the egg in a spiral over the whole surface, cover the pan, and give it forty-five to sixty seconds over a medium heat. Then take the lid off, pour the remaining third over the top, put the lid back on and count to twenty. Kill the heat. The bottom layer is now set and holding everything together; the top layer is barely cooked, glossy and still moving — and that contrast between set and almost-liquid in the same mouthful is the entire dish. Do not be tempted to give it another thirty seconds because it looks underdone. It IS underdone, on purpose, and it will carry on cooking on its way to the table. Have the rice already in a deep bowl, hot. Slide the whole thing out of the pan in one piece, using the lid or a spatula to keep the layers intact rather than tipping it in, and let it sit on top of the rice without stirring it in. Scatter the spring onion, add the mitsuba if you have it, and a pinch of shichimi togarashi. Eat it immediately, and eat it with a spoon.","cookTime":"Prep 5 min · Cook 8 min","kcal":"~610–700 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g, Carbs 74g, Fat 16g, Sodium ~1250mg","storage":"Honestly, do not keep it. The entire dish is a texture that exists for about ninety seconds, and a refrigerated oyakodon is set egg on cold rice, which is a different and much sadder thing. If you must, it keeps a day and is best reheated gently in a covered pan with a splash of dashi rather than in a microwave. The seasoning liquid — dashi, soy, mirin, sake and sugar — is worth making in a larger quantity and keeping: it is the base of half the donburi family and lasts a fortnight in the fridge.","chefNotes":"One bowl at a time in a small pan, never scaled up. Slice the onion with the grain. Beat the eggs badly — five strokes. Two pours, lid on, and stop while the top is still loose. It finishes in the bowl.","pairsWith":"A bowl of miso soup and something pickled and sharp — that is the whole meal and it wants nothing else. Green tea rather than beer.","trivia":"The name is oyako — parent and child — and it means precisely what you think it means: the chicken and the egg in the same bowl. It is printed on menus across Japan without comment, and it is one of the more startling pieces of matter-of-fact naming in any cuisine. The dish is usually traced to 1891 at Tamahide, a restaurant in Nihonbashi in Tokyo that specialised in shamo, a gamecock breed, served as hotpot. The story is that customers finishing a hotpot were in the habit of dipping the last of the chicken and broth into beaten egg and tipping it over rice, and that the proprietor's wife formalised the leftover ritual into a dish and put it on the menu. Tamahide is still trading and still queued for. What makes the story more than a nice anecdote is what it says about the whole donburi category, which is Japan's fast food and is built almost entirely on this logic: something savoury, cooked in a seasoned liquid, poured over a bowl of rice. Gyudon is beef, katsudon is a fried pork cutlet given the same egg treatment, tendon is tempura. They are cheap, they are fast, they are eaten standing up, and every one of them is the same idea — that rice is the meal and the thing on top of it is the seasoning, which is close to the opposite of how a Western plate is put together.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid and finding the top still wobbling — and knowing to take it off the heat anyway, then that first spoonful where set egg, loose egg, sweet onion and hot rice all arrive at once.","leftovers":["(easy) The seasoning liquid is the real leftover here — make triple and keep it in a jar. It turns thin-sliced beef into gyudon, a fried cutlet into katsudon and a pan of mushrooms into a very good vegetarian bowl, all in under ten minutes.","(easy) Any leftover cooked chicken and onion, chopped small, folded into fried rice with a beaten egg the next morning — the seasoning is already done and it needs nothing.","(creative) Spread cold leftovers into a small oiled pan, pour a fresh beaten egg over and cook it flat like a thick omelette, then cut it into wedges cold for a lunchbox. It is a real thing in Japan and it travels far better than the original bowl does."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mushroom & Tofu Oyakodon (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"Woodier and darker than the original, with mushrooms that have gone silky in the sweet broth and the same trembling egg on top.","trivia":"The name stops making literal sense the moment the chicken leaves, and Japanese menus are relaxed about this — a version of this bowl made with mushrooms and tofu is common in temple-influenced cooking and simply gets called a mushroom donburi. There is a real technical gain as well as a saving: mushrooms give up their own liquid into the broth, so the sauce ends up deeper and more savoury than the chicken version manages in the same time. Use two or three kinds if you can — a firm one for bite and a soft one for silkiness — and fry them hard in a dry pan first, before any liquid, so their water drives off and they brown rather than stew. The tofu wants to go in late and be left alone.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"120g chicken thighs, boned, cut into 2cm pieces","to":"140g mixed mushrooms, thickly sliced"},{"from":"120ml dashi","to":"120ml kombu and shiitake dashi"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g firm tofu, cut into 2cm cubes"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Dry-fry the mushrooms hard in the empty pan first, with no oil and no liquid, for 4 to 5 minutes until they have given up their water, it has boiled away, and they are browning at the edges. This step is not optional — mushrooms added straight to the broth stew and go grey. Set them aside, then build the seasoned liquid with the onion exactly as written, return the mushrooms to it with the tofu cubes, and simmer 3 minutes. Handle the tofu as little as possible from that point; it wants to be nudged, not stirred. Then the two egg pours and the lid, unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Classic Oyakodon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30},{"name":"Katsudon — the Fried Cutlet Version","icon":"🍖","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"A crumbed cutlet half-drowned in sweet broth and soft egg, one edge still crisp above the waterline — the most comforting bowl in Japan and not remotely a coincidence.","trivia":"Katsudon is oyakodon with a fried pork cutlet in place of the chicken, and it has a peculiar cultural life: it is the traditional thing a Japanese student eats the night before an exam, because katsu is a homophone of the verb to win, and it is such a fixture of police dramas as the meal offered to a suspect in an interrogation room that the trope is instantly recognisable. Technically there is exactly one thing to get right and it is timing. The cutlet is already cooked and already crisp, and every second it spends in liquid is a second of that crispness lost — so it goes in sliced, at the very last moment, for barely a minute before the egg, and the egg goes over it rather than around it. Done properly you get a cutlet that is soaked and soft underneath and still audibly crisp along its top edge, in the same mouthful. Done slowly you get wet breadcrumbs.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"120g chicken thighs, boned, cut into 2cm pieces","to":"1 tonkatsu, fried and sliced into 2cm strips"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry a tonkatsu exactly as its own card describes and slice it, then let it rest on a rack while you make the broth — do not let it sit on a plate, where the underside will go soft before it ever reaches the pan. Simmer the onion in the seasoned liquid as written, then lay the sliced cutlet in, keeping the slices together in their original shape, and give it 45 to 60 seconds only. Then straight to the two egg pours and the lid. The whole point is a cutlet that is soft where the broth reached it and still crisp along the top, so the shorter the contact the better — err on the side of too fast rather than too long."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-gyoza","name":"Gyoza","nameAlt":"Yaki-gyoza — pan-fried dumplings with a lace skirt","aliases":["Gyoza","Yaki Gyoza","Japanese Pan-Fried Dumplings","Potstickers"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","main","snack"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","party","family"],"ingredients":"90g white cabbage, finely chopped · 2g fine salt (for the cabbage) · 100g pork mince (20% fat) · 15g garlic chives, finely sliced · 5g garlic, grated · 4g fresh ginger, grated · 8ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 3ml sake · 2g sugar · 1g white pepper · 10 gyoza wrappers, the thinnest you can find · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g plain flour · 100ml water · 15ml rice vinegar · 8ml soy sauce, to dip · 2ml chilli oil, to dip","method":"Gyoza are two separate skills wearing one name: a filling that must be dry, and a fry that must be committed to. Get those right and everything else is folding practice. Start with the cabbage, because the cabbage is the dish's one real enemy. Chop it as finely as you have patience for — 2 to 3mm, not shredded and not minced to mush — then toss it with the 2g of salt in a bowl and walk away for fifteen minutes. It will slump and go glassy and a puddle will appear underneath it. Now gather it in both hands and WRING IT OUT properly, over the sink, squeezing until it genuinely hurts your hands and no more liquid comes; you should end up with a tight, damp little ball that has lost close to a third of its weight. Throw that liquid away. This is the single step that separates a gyoza that seals, fries and holds together from one that steams itself soggy from the inside, splits along the seam and spits fat at you the moment it hits the pan. It also seasons the cabbage all the way through, which is why no more salt appears anywhere in this recipe. Now the filling. Put the wrung cabbage in a bowl with the pork mince, garlic chives, grated garlic and ginger, soy, sesame oil, sake, sugar and white pepper, and mix it with your hand until it is completely uniform and starting to feel tacky rather than loose. Do not overwork it into a paste — you want it homogenous, not emulsified. Chill it for twenty minutes if you have time, because a cold filling is far easier to fold. Take a moment on the wrappers, because Japan's wrapper is the reason a gyoza is not a jiaozi. A Japanese gyoza wrapper is noticeably thinner than a Chinese boiling wrapper — machine-rolled thin, almost translucent at the edge — and the reason is simple: this dumpling is fried, not boiled, and A THICK WRAPPER WILL NEVER CRISP. It just turns leathery and chewy and no amount of extra pan time fixes it, because the problem is thickness, not colour. Buy the thinnest packet you can find, keep them under a damp cloth while you work, and if the packet has been in the freezer let them come fully to room temperature or they will crack along every fold. To fold: a rounded teaspoon of filling in the centre, wet the edge of the far half with a fingertip of water, fold into a half moon and pinch it shut at the top point. Then, working from one end, make five or six small pleats in the front side only, pressing each into the flat back side as you go — the back stays smooth, the pleats are all on the face. This is not decoration. Pleating on one side only is what pulls the dumpling into a curve and gives it a FLAT BASE that stands up on its own, and a gyoza that stands is a gyoza that fries evenly. As you finish each one, press it lightly down onto the board to set that flat bottom and stand it on a floured tray. Now the fry, and once it starts you are committed. Mix the 5g of flour into the 100ml of water and stir it until it is smooth and milky — this is the hane, the lace skirt, and it is the reason Japanese gyoza look the way they do. Heat the sunflower oil in a non-stick or well-seasoned frying pan over a medium-high heat until it shimmers, then arrange the gyoza flat-side down in a tight spiral or a fan, all of them touching. Arrange them properly now, because ONCE THE LIQUID GOES IN YOU CANNOT MOVE THEM. Fry undisturbed for two minutes until the bases are properly golden — lift one to check, and do not go early. Then stir the flour-water once more and pour it in around them until it comes about a third of the way up, clap the lid on and turn the heat down to medium. Six to seven minutes, and leave the lid alone. The wrappers will go translucent and puff slightly. When the loose liquid has gone, take the lid off, turn the heat back up and let it finish. Here is where nerve is required: THE SKIRT SHOULD GO PAST GOLDEN, well past — it is a sheet of cooked starch, not a dumpling, and it wants to be deep brown, brittle and lacy before you stop. Everyone pulls it too early. You will hear the sound in the pan change from a wet bubbling to a dry, high crackle and you will see the lace lift very slightly at the edges when you tilt the pan; that is it. Kill the heat, put a plate face down over the pan, and invert the whole thing in one confident movement so they land skirt-side UP, joined in a single golden disc. Mix the rice vinegar, dipping soy and chilli oil in a small saucer — sharper than you would expect, because it is cutting through a great deal of fat and garlic — and eat them immediately, while the lace still shatters.","cookTime":"Prep 40 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~520–600 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 26g, Carbs 48g, Fat 28g, Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Uncooked gyoza freeze beautifully and this is the real reason to make a big batch: freeze them on the floured tray, spaced apart, until solid, then bag them. They go into the pan straight from frozen with no thawing at all — same method, two extra minutes under the lid — and they are genuinely as good as fresh. Uncooked and unfrozen they will not keep, because the wet filling starts softening the wrappers within a couple of hours and they weld to the tray. Cooked ones keep two days in the fridge; reheat in a dry pan base-down with a splash of water and the lid on, never in a microwave, which turns the wrapper to putty.","chefNotes":"Salt the cabbage and wring it until it hurts. Thinnest wrappers you can buy. Pleat one side only so the base is flat. Arrange them before the liquid goes in, because after it you cannot move them. Let the lace skirt go darker than feels safe.","pairsWith":"Cold beer, and nothing else pretending to be a starter. If it is the meal, a bowl of rice and something sharply pickled.","trivia":"Gyoza came home in the kit bags of repatriated soldiers, and Japan adopted the wrong version on purpose. Dumplings in northern China are overwhelmingly boiled; pan-frying them is what you do with yesterday's leftovers, which is why the Chinese name for potstickers refers to the pan. Japan skipped the original entirely — there is essentially no boiled-gyoza culture, and yaki-gyoza is simply what the word means. The route is documented: after 1945 several million Japanese civilians and soldiers were repatriated from Manchuria and northern China, and the ones who had learned to cook there came back to a country with almost no food and set up stalls with what they knew. The two cities that became gyoza towns, Utsunomiya and Hamamatsu, are both places tied to those returning divisions, and they now trade the national top spot for household gyoza spending every year, a statistic reported with the seriousness of a sports result. The garlic is the other Japanese fingerprint. Northern Chinese jiaozi frequently contain none, and are eaten with raw garlic on the side instead. Japanese gyoza is built on it, which is exactly why it was for decades a slightly disreputable thing — food for after drinking, not before work.","howThisFeels":"That moment of nerve at the end, waiting for the lace to go one shade darker than feels wise — then the plate inverted over the pan, and a whole golden disc landing in one piece.","leftovers":["(easy) Any filling left over after the wrappers run out is a meatball mix. Roll it into small balls, fry them and simmer them for five minutes in a splash of soy, water and sugar.","(easy) Cooked leftover gyoza, chopped in half and dropped into a bowl of hot broth with noodles, are excellent — the wrapper softens back into something dumpling-like and the fried base holds.","(creative) Fry cold gyoza base-down in a little oil, then pour a beaten egg with a spoonful of the flour-water over the whole panful and let it set into a flat omelette around them. Cut into wedges."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Chicken & Spring Onion (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":17,"howThisFeels":"Lighter and cleaner in the mouth, with the garlic doing more of the talking because there is less fat to hide behind.","trivia":"Chicken mince is leaner than pork and that is a problem, not a saving: fat is what keeps the filling juicy while the wrapper crisps, and a lean gyoza goes dry and grainy. The fix is not more oil in the pan but more moisture inside — keep a little more of the cabbage water in this version by wringing slightly less hard, and add a teaspoon of cold water to the mince as you mix. Chicken thigh mince, if you can get it, solves it outright.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"100g pork mince (20% fat)","to":"100g chicken mince, thigh if possible"},{"from":"15g garlic chives, finely sliced","to":"15g spring onion, finely sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Wring the cabbage a touch less aggressively than the classic, and mix 5ml of cold water into the chicken along with the seasonings. Lean mince has no fat reserve to keep it juicy while the wrapper fries, so the moisture has to be put in deliberately. Everything else is unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Classic Pork & Garlic Chive","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":25,"howThisFeels":"Garlic and chive hitting you before the plate has landed — the smell that is the entire reason gyoza is drinking food.","trivia":"Nira, garlic chives, are the specific green here and they are not interchangeable with spring onion or ordinary chives. They are flatter, softer and far more pungent, they collapse into the filling rather than staying stringy, and their flavour is closer to garlic than to onion. Most Asian grocers carry them and they grow like a weed in a South African garden, coming back every year from the same clump.","delta":{}},{"name":"Shiitake & Tofu (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"Deep and woody rather than rich, and the one people go back for without noticing what is missing.","trivia":"Vegetable gyoza fail for the opposite reason meat ones do: not too much water, but no binder and no fat. Firm tofu crumbled and squeezed replaces the body, dried shiitake replaces the savoury depth, and a spoonful of sesame paste replaces the fat that carries it. Fry the mushrooms hard before they go in rather than adding them raw, because raw mushrooms release water inside the wrapper at exactly the wrong moment. Almost all commercial gyoza wrappers are flour, water and salt only, but check the packet for egg.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"100g pork mince (20% fat)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"12g dried shiitake mushrooms, soaked and finely chopped"},{"item":"90g firm tofu, crumbled and squeezed dry"},{"item":"8g white sesame paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Soak the dried shiitake in warm water for twenty minutes, squeeze them out and chop them finely, keeping a tablespoon of the strained soaking liquid. Fry them hard in a dry pan for three or four minutes until they squeak and start to brown — raw mushroom lets go of its water inside the wrapper, which is precisely where you do not want it. Crumble the tofu into a clean cloth and wring it out the same way you wrung the cabbage. Mix the tofu, fried mushrooms, sesame paste and the reserved spoonful of soaking liquid into the cabbage with all the seasonings, and fold as normal. The mix is looser than a meat filling, so chill it properly before you fold or it will fight you."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"},{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"}]},
{"id":"japan-chawanmushi","name":"Chawanmushi","nameAlt":"Savoury steamed egg custard, eaten with a spoon","aliases":["Chawanmushi","Chawan Mushi","Japanese Steamed Egg Custard","Savoury Egg Custard"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","light"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","special","family"],"ingredients":"1 egg, about 50g out of the shell · 150ml dashi · 5ml light soy sauce · 5ml mirin · 3ml sake · 1g fine salt · 30g chicken thigh, in 1cm pieces · 2 prawns, peeled and deveined · 1 fresh shiitake mushroom, thinly sliced · 15g kamaboko fish cake, sliced · 3 sprigs mitsuba, or 5g spring onion greens","method":"This is a recipe with exactly one number in it, and everything else is decoration. THE RATIO IS THREE PARTS DASHI TO ONE PART EGG, BY WEIGHT — and you weigh the egg, which is a strange instruction in a home kitchen and the reason most chawanmushi go wrong. Eggs are not a unit of measurement. A large egg gives about 50g out of the shell and a small one gives closer to 40, so a recipe saying 'one egg to 150ml of stock' is quietly asking for anywhere between 3:1 and nearly 4:1 without telling you. Below about two and a half to one you have not made a custard, you have made a firm savoury omelette standing up in a cup. Above about four to one it will not set at all, and no extra steaming time will rescue it — you will simply have hot seasoned soup with an egg in it. So crack the egg into a small bowl on the scale, read the number, and multiply it by three. That is your dashi quantity. Now season the liquid, and season it before the egg goes anywhere near it, because THIS IS THE ONLY MOMENT YOU CAN TASTE IT. Warm the dashi very gently with the soy, mirin, sake and salt just until the salt dissolves, then take it off and LET IT COOL to no more than lukewarm. Hot dashi poured into raw egg starts cooking it on contact in fine ribbons, and those ribbons will not dissolve, will not strain out and will sit in the finished custard as visible threads. Taste the seasoned dashi now. It should taste like a slightly under-salted clear soup you would happily drink — remember that it is about to be diluted by a third with unseasoned egg, so it needs to be a shade stronger than you want the finished dish. Beat the egg gently with chopsticks or a fork, cutting through it rather than whipping, until the white is fully broken up and no clear jelly remains, but stopping well before it froths. Pour the cooled dashi in, stir to combine, then pass the whole thing through a fine sieve into a jug. Now build the cups, and the rule for the fillings is short: EVERYTHING GOING IN MUST ALREADY BE COOKED, CUT SMALL AND COOLED. The custard sets in twelve minutes at a temperature deliberately kept low, and nothing raw cooks properly in that window. A raw prawn dropped in will release its water into the setting custard and leave a wet hollow around itself; raw chicken is worse and is a food-safety problem as well as a texture one. So poach the chicken pieces and the prawns for a minute in a little dashi or salted water, drain them, and let them cool. Divide the chicken, prawns, sliced shiitake and kamaboko between two small heatproof cups or one large one, then pour the egg mixture over, filling to about two centimetres below the rim. Skim off any bubbles on the surface with the corner of a piece of kitchen paper. NOW COVER EACH CUP — a small lid, or a square of foil pressed on. This is not to keep heat in. It is because a steamer lid drips, and a single drop of condensation landing on setting custard leaves a crater in the surface; a chawanmushi is judged on being a flat mirror, and a pockmarked one has been photographed with its mistakes. Get the steamer going first so it is already at full steam, then turn it down until the water is at a bare, lazy simmer before the cups go in. This custard wants around 85°C, not 100, and a hard rolling steam blows it up into a bubbled honeycomb that weeps liquid the moment your spoon goes in — the failure looks like a sponge and it is not reversible. Wedge a chopstick under the steamer lid to let some heat escape and keep the pot honest. Steam for twelve to fourteen minutes for small cups. The test is not a wobble, it is a skewer: push a thin skewer or a knife tip in at the edge and watch what wells up. IF THE LIQUID THAT RISES IS CLEAR, IT IS DONE. If it is cloudy and yellow, that is uncooked egg and it needs another two minutes. Lay the mitsuba or spring onion on top for the last thirty seconds, just long enough to wilt and turn a deeper green. Serve it hot, in its cup, with a spoon.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 14 min","kcal":"~170–210 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g, Carbs 4g, Fat 8g, Sodium ~950mg","storage":"Two days covered in the fridge, and it is one of the rare custards that is genuinely good cold — served chilled in summer it is a completely legitimate version, not a compromise. Do not reheat it in a microwave under any circumstances: the custard passes straight from set to curdled and separates into scrambled egg sitting in a puddle. If you want it hot again, put the covered cup back in a gentle steamer for four or five minutes. The seasoned dashi base keeps a week in the fridge and is worth making in quantity, because it is also the liquid for udon broth and for simmered vegetables.","chefNotes":"Weigh the egg and use three times its weight in dashi. Season and taste the dashi before the egg goes in — after that you are blind. Everything inside must be cooked and cool. Cover each cup or the steamer will drip craters into it. Bare simmer, never a hard steam. Skewer test: clear liquid means done.","pairsWith":"It opens a meal rather than joining one — a cup of it before grilled fish and rice, or alongside a tray of sashimi. Warm sake or green tea.","trivia":"It is one of the very few dishes in Japanese cuisine eaten with a spoon, and that spoon is the fingerprint of a foreign parent. A Japanese place setting is chopsticks, and dishes are designed around them; a bowl of soup is drunk straight from the bowl. Chawanmushi arrives with a spoon because it came in through Nagasaki, the single port left open during the two centuries of national seclusion, where Chinese and Dutch traders were confined and where a hybrid banquet cuisine called shippoku developed — Japanese ingredients on a round Chinese table with shared dishes and, notably, spoons. The restaurant generally credited with putting it on a menu, Yossou in Nagasaki, has been serving it since 1866 and is still open. The name is the other tell: chawan means tea bowl and mushi means steamed, so the dish is named for its container rather than its contents, because it was originally made in whatever teacup was to hand. The lidded, purpose-made chawanmushi cups sold today are a later formalisation of a dish whose whole original character was improvisation.","howThisFeels":"The lid coming off a small warm cup, and a surface so flat it reflects the ceiling — then the spoon going through it with almost no resistance at all.","leftovers":["(easy) Eat it cold from the fridge with a little grated ginger on top. In summer this is not a compromise, it is how it is served.","(easy) The seasoned dashi base, made in triple quantity, is the broth for udon and the simmering liquid for pumpkin or daikon — it lasts a week and saves the seasoning step entirely.","(creative) Break up leftover custard into a bowl of hot udon broth. It fragments into soft savoury clouds and turns a plain noodle bowl into something that tastes far more considered than it was."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mushroom & Spring Onion (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Quieter and woodier, and somehow more comforting for having nothing in it to chase.","trivia":"Without egg there is nothing to set, so this version stops being a custard and becomes a savoury silken pudding — the mechanism is different but the mouthfeel lands in the same place. Silken tofu blitzed smooth carries the body, and a small amount of kudzu or cornflour does the setting. The ratio law still applies in spirit: too much liquid and it slumps, too little and it is tofu in a cup. The dashi must be the kombu-and-shiitake version, and it should be a strong one, because there is no egg richness to lean on.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"1 egg, about 50g out of the shell"},{"item":"30g chicken thigh, in 1cm pieces"},{"item":"2 prawns, peeled and deveined"},{"item":"15g kamaboko fish cake, sliced"}],"addIng":[{"item":"120g silken tofu"},{"item":"8g cornflour"},{"item":"30g mixed mushrooms, sliced and fried"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use the kombu-and-shiitake dashi and make it stronger than usual — soak the kombu overnight and use double the dried shiitake — because there is no egg or bonito to carry it. Blitz the silken tofu completely smooth with the cornflour and 100ml of the cooled seasoned dashi, then sieve it, which matters more here than with egg because tofu holds grain. Fry the mushrooms hard first and let them cool. Steam exactly as the classic, covered, at a bare simmer, for fourteen to sixteen minutes. The skewer test does not work on this one — instead, tilt the cup gently: a set pudding moves as a single mass, an unset one ripples."}]}},{"name":"Classic Chawanmushi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"Finding a prawn at the bottom of the cup you had entirely forgotten was coming.","trivia":"Mitsuba is worth hunting down rather than substituting. It looks like flat-leaf parsley and tastes like nothing else — somewhere between celery leaf, chervil and coriander stem — and it is one of the very small handful of herbs native to Japan rather than imported. Ginkgo nuts are the other classic inclusion, small, jade-green and faintly bitter, sold tinned; they are a genuinely acquired taste and leaving them out is not a failure of authenticity.","delta":{}},{"name":"Odamaki Mushi — with Udon (Osaka)","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"Spoon going in expecting custard and hitting noodles — a starter that turns out to have been dinner all along.","trivia":"Odamaki mushi is chawanmushi with a nest of udon hidden underneath, and it is an Osaka speciality that turns a delicate opening course into an entire winter meal in one bowl. The name refers to a ball of wound thread, which is what the coiled noodles look like through the custard. It needs a bigger bowl and a longer steam, and the noodles must be fully cooked and rinsed cold first, because a dry noodle drinks the custard and you end up with a bowl of stodge. It is also the version that proves the ratio law from the other direction: use the same 3:1 mix and simply pour more of it.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"120g cooked udon noodles, rinsed and drained"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use one large bowl rather than small cups, and increase the egg-and-dashi mix by half — weighing the egg and multiplying by three exactly as before, because the ratio does not change, only the quantity. Cook the udon, rinse it under cold water until it is completely cold and drain it very well; a warm or wet noodle bleeds starch and water into the custard. Coil it into the bottom of the bowl, arrange the chicken, prawns and mushroom on top of it, and pour the mixture over. Cover and steam at the same bare simmer for twenty to twenty-two minutes — the extra depth needs the extra time, and the skewer test at the edge is still the only reliable one."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Gyoza","target":"japan-gyoza","emoji":"🥟"}]},
{"id":"japan-yakitori","name":"Yakitori","nameAlt":"Charcoal-grilled chicken skewers — negima, shio and tare","aliases":["Yakitori","Negima","Japanese Chicken Skewers","Grilled Chicken Skewers"],"course":"main","type":["main","starter","grill"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","party","family","braai"],"ingredients":"180g chicken thighs, boneless with the skin on, in 25g pieces · 60g spring onions or baby leeks, in 3cm lengths · 15g chicken wing tips or bones · 45ml Japanese soy sauce · 45ml mirin · 20ml sake · 12g sugar · 2g fine sea salt · 1g sansho pepper · 5ml sunflower oil · 4 bamboo skewers, soaked 30 minutes","method":"Yakitori looks like the simplest thing on any Japanese menu and is in fact a set of small decisions made in the right order, none of which is difficult and all of which show. Start by making the tare, because it wants half an hour and it improves while you do everything else. Grill or dry-fry the chicken wing tips or bones hard in a small pan until they are properly browned all over — this is where the tare's depth comes from and pale bones give a thin, one-note sauce. Add the soy, mirin, sake and sugar, bring it to a simmer and let it reduce gently for twenty-five to thirty minutes until it coats a spoon and falls off it slowly, like thin cream. Strain it and set it aside; it will thicken further as it cools, so stop it looser than you want it. Now the skewering, and this is where most home yakitori is lost. THE SKEWER IS A COOKING TOOL, NOT A WAY OF CARRYING FOOD TO THE TABLE. Cut the thigh into pieces of genuinely equal thickness — not equal weight, equal thickness, because thickness is what determines cooking time and a fat piece next to a thin one guarantees that one of them is wrong. Thread them so that they TOUCH BUT ARE NOT COMPRESSED. A gap between pieces exposes two cut faces to direct heat and they dry out and go grey; pieces jammed hard together shield each other so completely that the middle is still raw when the outsides are done. They should sit against each other the way books sit on a shelf. For the classic negima, alternate chicken and spring onion, and understand that the onion is not there for colour: it releases steam right beside the chicken as it cooks, which keeps the meat from drying, and a 3cm length of spring onion happens to cook in almost exactly the time a 25g piece of thigh does. Fold the skin outward on each piece rather than tucking it in, so it faces the fire and has a chance to crisp. Now the fire, and here is the law that decides everything: YOU ARE COOKING OVER RADIANT HEAT AND THERE MUST BE NO FLAME. Let the coals burn all the way down until they are covered in grey ash and glowing steadily with nothing visible above them. Chicken thigh with the skin on drips fat continuously, fat meeting flame produces soot, and soot lands on the meat and tastes exactly like what it is. If a flare-up starts, move the skewers — DO NOT SPRAY IT WITH WATER, which lifts a cloud of ash straight onto the food and puts out the part of the fire you needed. Set the skewers close to the coals, about 8 to 10cm, and lay the exposed wooden ends over the edge of the grill or shield them with a strip of foil so they do not burn through. Turn them every minute or so. You want six to eight minutes total for thigh, and the test is the fat: when the skin has stopped weeping and has gone from soft to visibly tight and glossy, the piece is nearly there. Now the seasoning decision, which is not a matter of taste but of the cut, and every yakitori-ya in Japan makes it the same way. SHIO — SALT — IS FOR LEAN AND DELICATE THINGS, TARE IS FOR FAT AND STRONG ONES. Breast, wing and gizzard go shio, because tare is sweet and dark and would simply bury them. Thigh, skin and liver take tare, because they have the fat and the flavour to stand up to it. And the timing of each is opposite: SHIO GOES ON BEFORE, salted lightly the moment the skewers are threaded so it has time to draw and season; TARE GOES ON AT THE END AND NEVER AT THE START. Tare is a third sugar by the time it has reduced, and sugar over live coals burns black in under a minute, so it goes on only for the last third of the cooking, in THREE THIN COATS with a turn between each — brush, turn, thirty seconds, brush, turn, thirty seconds, brush, turn, off. Three thin layers build a lacquer that clings; one thick layer slides off into the fire and takes the flavour with it. Brush from a small bowl decanted from the main tare, and never dip a raw skewer into the master pot — the reason a yakitori shop can keep the same tare going for years is that only cooked meat ever touches it. Serve the shio skewers with a pinch of sansho pepper, which is a citrus-scented relative of the Sichuan peppercorn and does for salt-grilled chicken what lemon does for fish, and the tare skewers straight off the grill while the lacquer is still tacky. Eat them with your fingers, off the skewer, standing up if possible.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 30 min tare + 8 min grill","kcal":"~480–560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 42g, Carbs 22g, Fat 26g, Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"The tare is the thing worth keeping and it is close to indestructible: it is a reduced, sugared, salted, alcohol-cooked liquid, which is to say it is a preserve. Keep it in a clean jar in the fridge and it will hold for a month or more, deepening as it goes; top it up with a fresh batch rather than starting again, and every time you make yakitori it gets better, because each grilling adds a little rendered chicken fat and juice back into it. Bring it to a simmer once a fortnight if it is being topped up regularly. Cooked skewers keep two days and are best revived under a hot grill for a minute rather than in a pan — they were cooked by radiant heat and they reheat best the same way.","chefNotes":"Equal thickness, not equal weight. Pieces touching but never compressed. Grey coals, no flame, and never water on a flare-up. Salt goes on before; tare goes on only in the last third, in three thin coats with a turn between each. Decant the tare you brush with — nothing raw ever touches the main pot.","pairsWith":"Cold beer, ice-cold, and a bowl of cabbage wedges with a salty dip to eat between skewers. Rice only if it is the whole meal rather than the start of one.","trivia":"The fuel is the famous part, and it explains the equipment. Binchotan is a white charcoal made from ubame oak, fired at very high temperature and then smothered in a damp mix of ash and sand, which is what leaves the pale coating that gives it its name. What comes out is close to pure carbon: it burns with almost no smoke and no flame at all, holds around a thousand degrees, and lasts for hours. Two pieces struck together ring like metal rather than knocking like wood, and that sound is part of how it is graded. Because it gives radiant infrared heat rather than smoke and hot air, the grill built for it is long, narrow and open, with no lid anywhere in sight — the entire geometry of a yakitori counter follows from the properties of the charcoal, and a lid would be actively wrong. It is also expensive enough to be a genuine line item in a small restaurant's costs, which is why a good yakitori-ya charges what it does for what is, on paper, a few pieces of chicken on a stick.","howThisFeels":"The particular hiss and sweet smell the moment tare hits hot coals — and knowing you have about thirty seconds before it goes from lacquer to charcoal.","leftovers":["(easy) Pull cold leftover chicken off the skewers and stir it through hot rice with a spoonful of the tare and a beaten egg — a two-minute donburi that tastes like it took effort.","(easy) The tare is the leftover that matters. It glazes salmon, pork belly and aubergine, and it is the base of a very good marinade for a braai.","(creative) Chop leftover skewers small, fold them into a cabbage-heavy okonomiyaki batter, and use the tare in place of the usual sauce."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Thigh & Spring Onion, Grill Pan (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":27,"howThisFeels":"Kitchen window open, extractor on full, and the smell going through the whole house anyway.","trivia":"Being honest about what is lost: without coals there is no radiant infrared heat and no smoke, so this will taste of grilled chicken and tare rather than of fire. What you can recover is the char, and the way to get it is a ridged cast-iron pan taken hotter than feels sensible and left completely alone between turns. Do not crowd it — three skewers at a time, not six — because a crowded pan drops in temperature and the chicken poaches in its own juices instead of searing. The oven grill is the second-best option, on the top shelf, with the door slightly ajar so the element stays on continuously rather than cycling off.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"15g chicken wing tips or bones"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the tare without the bones — reduce the soy, mirin, sake and sugar for fifteen minutes instead of thirty. It will be simpler and slightly sweeter, so pull it a little short. Get a ridged cast-iron pan properly hot over a high heat, wipe it with the oil, and lay the skewers on with plenty of space between them. Leave them undisturbed for two minutes a side for four turns; every time you move them early you lose the crust. Brush the tare on in the last ninety seconds only, in the same three thin coats, and expect a certain amount of smoke and honest sticking — that caramelised residue in the pan is worth deglazing with a splash of water and pouring back over."}]}},{"name":"Classic Negima over Coals","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":40,"howThisFeels":"Standing over the coals turning skewers while everyone else is inside, which is the correct place to be.","trivia":"Negima is written with the characters for spring onion and for the gaps between things, and the second half of the name is a small joke that has outlived its own origin: the original negima was spring onion and tuna, ma being short for maguro, and chicken replaced the fish somewhere in the early twentieth century when tuna got expensive and chicken got cheap. The name did not change. A dish named after an ingredient it no longer contains is more common than it should be.","delta":{}},{"name":"The Yakitori-ya Spread — Skin, Liver & Tsukune","icon":"🍢","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":58,"howThisFeels":"Six different skewers arriving one at a time, each one a different texture, and realising nobody at the table has spoken for ten minutes.","trivia":"A yakitori-ya buys whole birds and puts every part of them on a stick, and the menu is essentially a map of a chicken. Kawa is the skin alone, wound tightly onto the skewer and cooked long and slow until it renders down to something between crackling and toffee — it takes fifteen minutes rather than eight and it cannot be rushed. Reba is liver, grilled just to pink inside and no further. Tsukune is a minced patty, and it is the one item on the list that exists to use up everything the other skewers could not: trim, cartilage, off-cuts. The raw-egg-yolk dip that comes with it is a Japanese convention that assumes a national egg supply graded for raw eating, so treat it as optional here. Nothing on the bird is thrown away, and the whole-bird economy is exactly why these shops can charge what they do for thigh and still make sense.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g chicken skin, in strips"},{"item":"60g chicken livers, trimmed"},{"item":"80g chicken mince with 5g grated ginger, for tsukune"},{"item":"10g shiso or perilla leaves"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Wind the skin strips tightly onto their own skewer, concertina fashion, and give them fifteen minutes at the cooler end of the coals rather than eight at the hot end — skin needs its fat rendered out slowly and then crisped, and hurrying it gives you something rubbery that will not improve. Skewer the livers whole, salt them, and cook them fast and briefly: four minutes, turning often, and off while the centre is still just pink, because a fully cooked liver goes chalky and there is no coming back. For the tsukune, work the mince with the ginger and a pinch of salt in one direction until it goes tacky and holds together, then mould it around the skewers in a long oval and grill it eight minutes, brushing with tare for the last two. Serve the liver shio, the skin shio, and the tsukune tare — the seasoning follows the cut, as always."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Gyoza","target":"japan-gyoza","emoji":"🥟"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-tempura","name":"Tempura","nameAlt":"Prawn and vegetable tempura with tentsuyu","aliases":["Tempura","Tempura Prawns","Ebi Tempura","Japanese Battered Prawns"],"course":"main","type":["main","starter"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","special","family"],"ingredients":"4 large prawns, peeled with the tails left on · 60g sweet potato, in 5mm rounds · 40g aubergine · 30g green beans · 20g shiitake mushrooms · 60g cake flour · 15g cornflour · 1 egg yolk · 150ml iced water · 500ml sunflower oil · 100ml dashi · 25ml mirin · 25ml Japanese soy sauce · 30g daikon, finely grated · 3g fresh ginger, grated · 2g fine sea salt","method":"Tempura is not a batter recipe, it is a campaign against gluten, and once you understand that everything odd about the method becomes obvious. Flour plus water plus agitation makes gluten; gluten makes bread; bread is the exact opposite of what you are trying to produce. So every instruction below exists to stop the flour from ever properly becoming a batter. Prepare absolutely everything first, because from the moment the batter is mixed you have about ten minutes and no time to be looking for the sieve. Make the tentsuyu: warm the dashi, mirin and soy together just to a simmer, then take it off and let it cool to warm. Grate the daikon finely and squeeze it lightly — it goes into the dipping bowl at the table, and its job is enzymatic as much as it is flavour, cutting the richness of fried oil in a way that lemon does not. Grate the ginger. Now the ingredients, and the rule is that they must be BONE DRY and cut so they cook in the time the coating takes. Pat the prawns completely dry with kitchen paper. Water repels hot oil violently, so a wet prawn spits dangerously and, worse, the batter slides straight off it in the pan. Prawns curl into a tight comma as they cook because the connective tissue on the belly contracts faster than the muscle — the same problem the pork cutlet has, solved the same way, with three or four small snips across the belly and a firm press to straighten; that is a borrowed law rather than a new one, and it takes ten seconds. Slice the sweet potato into 5mm rounds — thicker and the inside will still be raw when the coating is dark. Cut the aubergine into a fan: lengthways slices leaving 1cm attached at the stem end, then press it open. Trim the beans and halve the shiitake. Dust everything very lightly with a little of the flour and tap most of it off, which gives the batter something to grip. Heat the oil in a deep, heavy pan to 180°C, filling it no more than a third full. If you have no thermometer, drop a little batter in once it is mixed: it should sink about halfway, hesitate, and rise back within two or three seconds. Straight to the bottom and staying there means too cool; sizzling furiously on the surface without sinking at all means too hot, and too hot gives you a dark coating around a raw centre. Only now, with the oil already at temperature, do you mix the batter. Sift the cake flour and cornflour together and put the bowl in the freezer for ten minutes if you have the time — COLD IS THE FIRST DEFENCE, because gluten forms more slowly at low temperature. Beat the egg yolk into the iced water in a separate bowl, then tip the cold flour in all at once and stir it with CHOPSTICKS, not a whisk, using ten to fifteen strokes and then stopping dead. THE BATTER MUST STILL LOOK WRONG. There should be visible lumps, and dry pockets of unmixed flour on the surface, and it should be thin enough that you can see the colour of a prawn through it once dipped. Every stroke past fifteen is developing gluten you cannot undo, and a smooth, glossy, well-mixed tempura batter fries into something heavy and bready that goes soft in three minutes. If it looks like pancake batter you have already lost. Fry in small batches, never covering more than a third of the oil's surface, because crowding drops the temperature and the coating absorbs oil instead of sealing against it. Dip a piece, let the excess run off for a second, and lay it into the oil away from you. Vegetables want a slightly cooler oil, around 165 to 170°C, because they need time to actually cook through; prawns and seafood want it hotter, 180 to 190°C, because they need speed. Sweet potato takes about three minutes, aubergine and mushroom two, beans ninety seconds, prawns ninety seconds to two minutes and not a second more. Here is the flourish worth learning: as each piece cooks, dip your fingertips in the batter and FLICK a few droplets over it in the oil. They set instantly onto the surface as spiky, brittle little flowers — the Japanese phrase is to make the flowers bloom — and that ragged, uneven surface is what gives good tempura its shattering texture and its silhouette. Skim the loose bits between batches or they burn and darken the oil. Lift each piece out and stand it on a rack, never on paper, and never stacked; a rack lets steam escape from underneath, and a piece lying flat on kitchen paper steams its own base soft within a minute. Salt it immediately, while it is hot enough for the salt to stick. And then serve it AT ONCE. This is a dish timed to the person eating rather than to the kitchen, which is precisely why a tempura counter cooks one piece at a time and hands it straight over, and why a large platter arriving all together has already begun to fail. Tentsuyu in a small bowl with the grated daikon and ginger stirred in; or simply salt, which for good prawns and sweet potato is arguably better.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~590–680 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g, Carbs 52g, Fat 34g, Sodium ~1050mg","storage":"Do not keep it. Tempura is a texture with a lifespan measured in minutes, and refrigerated tempura is a soft, oily thing that no oven fully rescues — the honest advice is to fry only what will be eaten. If you do have leftovers, do not reheat them as tempura: put them straight into the tendon or the noodle-bowl treatments below, where softening is part of the design rather than a fault. The frying oil, cooled and strained through a coffee filter, is good for two or three more sessions; keep it in a dark bottle and discard it once it darkens or starts to smell fishy. The tentsuyu keeps a week in the fridge.","chefNotes":"Everything bone dry and prepped before the batter exists. Oil to temperature first, batter second. Iced water, cold flour, chopsticks, fifteen strokes, and stop while it still looks lumpy and wrong. Never crowd the pan. Flick droplets on to make the flowers. Drain standing on a rack, salt at once, serve immediately.","pairsWith":"A bowl of plain rice, a small bowl of miso soup and something pickled — the standard set, and it wants nothing more elaborate. Cold beer or dry sake.","trivia":"Japan's most formal fried food began as street food, and it began there for a fire regulation. Edo in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries was a city built almost entirely of wood and paper, and it burned down with grim regularity; deep-frying over an open flame indoors was a genuine hazard and was pushed out of buildings and onto the street. So tempura was sold from mobile stalls, on skewers, eaten standing up, dipped into a communal pot of sauce that everybody at the stall shared — closer to a boerewors roll on a street corner than to anything with a tablecloth. That it is now among the most rarefied cuisines in Japan, with counters where a chef fries one piece at a time in front of you and an apprenticeship runs a decade, is one of the more complete reversals in food. The name itself came from further away still: Portuguese missionaries at Nagasaki in the sixteenth century, and most likely the Latin quatuor tempora, the ember days on which meat was forbidden and battered fried fish was what you ate instead. A religious restriction became a fast-food snack became a formal art, which is a long journey for a piece of prawn.","howThisFeels":"The sound when it is right — not a crunch but a shatter, like something thin breaking — and the flick of the fingertips that makes those spiky little flowers bloom in the oil.","leftovers":["(easy) Tendon: put yesterday's tempura on hot rice and pour over a sauce of 2 parts each dashi and mirin to 1 part soy, simmered five minutes. Softening tempura is the entire point of the dish, so leftovers are the correct ingredient.","(easy) Drop cold tempura into a bowl of hot udon or soba broth for thirty seconds. Tempura udon is a menu item precisely because a batter that has gone soft in broth is a good thing.","(creative) The loose fried batter bits skimmed from the oil are tenkasu and they are worth keeping in a jar — scattered over rice, noodles or okonomiyaki they add crunch and a little fried richness for nothing."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Vegetable Tempura (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":23,"howThisFeels":"A pile of ordinary vegetables turned briefly extraordinary, and gone within five minutes of hitting the plate.","trivia":"Vegetable tempura is not the compromise it sounds like — pumpkin, sweet potato, aubergine, green pepper, onion, mushroom and shiso are the traditional set and several of them are better than prawn. The two adjustments that matter are temperature and thickness: vegetables need to cook through rather than merely heat through, so the oil comes down to about 165°C and everything is cut thin. The egg yolk comes out and cold sparkling water goes in, which does most of the same job — the bubbles keep the batter light and the yolk's contribution was mostly colour. Use the kombu-and-shiitake dashi for the tentsuyu and it is vegan throughout.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"4 large prawns, peeled with the tails left on"},{"item":"1 egg yolk"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g pumpkin, in 4mm slices"},{"item":"40g onion, in thick rings"},{"item":"30g green pepper, in wide strips"},{"item":"150ml iced sparkling water"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the batter with iced sparkling water and no egg yolk, mixed the same way and just as badly — fifteen strokes with chopsticks, lumps left visible. The bubbles do what the yolk was doing for lightness. Cut everything thinner than you would for prawn tempura, 4 to 5mm, and drop the oil to 165°C so the inside cooks before the coating darkens. Pumpkin and sweet potato take three to four minutes, onion rings and peppers two, mushrooms and shiso leaves under a minute. Use the kombu-and-shiitake dashi in the tentsuyu."}]}},{"name":"Classic Prawn & Vegetable","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"That first prawn, straight from the rack, still too hot to hold properly and eaten anyway.","trivia":"Cake flour rather than plain is a real difference and not a fussy one: it has a lower protein content, which means less gluten available to form in the first place, so the batter is more forgiving of the extra stir you will inevitably give it. The cornflour has no gluten at all and dilutes the flour further. If you only have plain flour, replace a fifth of it with cornflour and be even stricter about the stirring. Sesame oil blended into the frying oil at about one part in five is what several old Tokyo tempura houses use, and it changes the smell of the whole dish.","delta":{}},{"name":"Kakiage & Cold Soba","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":46,"howThisFeels":"Breaking a shaggy golden disc apart with chopsticks over a tray of cold noodles on a hot afternoon.","trivia":"Kakiage is the one tempura that is an assembly rather than a single item: everything shredded fine, bound in a minimum of batter and fried as one shaggy fritter. It is where a Japanese kitchen puts the odds and ends — carrot ends, half an onion, a few small prawns — and it is deliberately loose and full of holes, because a densely packed kakiage fries into a solid puck. Served with cold soba on a bamboo tray it becomes ten-zaru, which is summer food: the noodles are dipped into a strong cold broth rather than sitting in it, and the last of the broth is traditionally topped up with the starchy water the soba was cooked in and drunk from the cup.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"120g dried soba noodles"},{"item":"50g carrot, in matchsticks"},{"item":"50g onion, thinly sliced"},{"item":"10g spring onion, sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Toss the shredded carrot, onion, spring onion and a few chopped prawns with a tablespoon of the dry flour until everything is lightly coated, then add only just enough batter to make it cling — far less than feels right. Slide a spoonful onto a flat perforated ladle or a square of baking paper and lower it into 170°C oil, then slip it off; it will hold its shape after twenty seconds. Do not press it down. Poke a few holes through it with a chopstick as it fries so the oil gets into the middle, and turn it once. Three to four minutes. Meanwhile boil the soba in plenty of unsalted water for the time on the packet, then rinse it under cold running water, rubbing the strands with your hands to wash off the surface starch, and drain it well. Make the dipping broth stronger than the tentsuyu — 3 parts dashi to 1 each of mirin and soy — and serve it cold in a cup beside the noodles, with the kakiage on the tray."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Chawanmushi","target":"japan-chawanmushi","emoji":"🥚"}]},
{"id":"japan-dorayaki","name":"Dorayaki","nameAlt":"Honey pancakes filled with sweet red bean paste","aliases":["Dorayaki","Red Bean Pancakes","Japanese Pancake Sandwich","Anko Pancakes"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","snack","baking"],"diet":["vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","party"],"ingredients":"80g dried azuki beans · 70g caster sugar, for the anko · 1g fine salt · 1 egg · 30g caster sugar · 12g honey · 5ml mirin · 45g cake flour · 1.5g bicarbonate of soda · 12ml water · 3ml sunflower oil","method":"Two things happen here and they are almost unrelated: a bean paste that takes two hours and rewards patience, and a pancake that takes four minutes and rewards restraint. Make the anko first, ideally the day before, because it is better cold and better still on its second day. Rinse the azuki beans and put them in a pan with plenty of cold water. Bring them to the boil, let them roll for three minutes, and then POUR THE WHOLE LOT AWAY down the sink. This is called shibukiri, cutting the astringency, and it is not squeamishness — azuki skins carry tannins and saponins that make a paste taste faintly harsh and drying, and that first water takes most of them out. Some cooks do it twice. Cover the drained beans with fresh cold water by about 4cm, bring them back to a simmer, and cook them uncovered for sixty to ninety minutes, topping up with hot water whenever they threaten to emerge — they must stay submerged the whole time or the exposed ones toughen and stay hard for good. Test by pressing a bean between finger and thumb: it must crush to a paste with almost no pressure, with no chalky core whatsoever. Now, and ONLY NOW, does the sugar go in. THIS IS THE LAW OF THE WHOLE PASTE: SUGAR ADDED TO BEANS THAT ARE NOT YET FULLY SOFT WILL KEEP THEM HARD FOREVER. Sugar in the surrounding water draws water back out of the beans by osmosis and firms their skins, and once that has happened another hour of boiling will not undo it. Every gritty, disappointing homemade anko is this mistake. So when the beans crush easily, drain off most of the water, keeping a cupful, add the sugar in two or three additions with the salt, and cook it down over a medium heat, stirring across the bottom of the pan, for fifteen to twenty minutes. Stop while it is still LOOSER THAN YOU WANT IT — it thickens substantially as it cools, and a paste reduced to the right consistency while hot sets into something you could build with. For a chunky tsubuan, mash it roughly with the back of the spoon and leave it there. For a smooth koshian, push it through a sieve, which is genuinely tedious and genuinely better. Cool it completely. The pancakes are a different discipline. Whisk the egg, the 30g of sugar, the honey and the mirin together until pale and slightly thickened — a minute of proper whisking, no more. The honey and mirin are not there to sweeten it. They are hygroscopic, meaning they hold onto water, and they are the entire reason a dorayaki is still soft three days later where a plain pancake is stale within the hour; the mirin also helps it brown evenly. Sift in the cake flour and the bicarbonate of soda and fold it through until it is just combined. Then REST THE BATTER, covered, in the fridge for at least thirty minutes and up to a couple of hours. The rest lets the flour hydrate completely and gives the bicarbonate time to start working, and a rested batter spreads into a smooth round on its own where an unrested one sits in a lumpy blob. It will have thickened while it rested, so stir the 12ml of water through it now to bring it back to the consistency of thick cream — it should fall off the spoon in a ribbon that sinks back within about three seconds. Heat a non-stick pan over a MEDIUM-LOW heat and give it a few minutes to reach an even temperature; a pan that is hot in the middle gives you a ring. Wipe it with a little oil on kitchen paper and then WIPE IT ALMOST DRY. Visible oil in the pan fries the surface unevenly and gives a blotchy, mottled face, and a dorayaki's face is the whole point of it. Pour the batter from a spoon, from one fixed point and one fixed height, and DO NOT SPREAD IT — let it find its own circle, about 8cm. Spreading it with the back of a spoon gives a thin ragged edge that cooks before the middle does. Now watch it, and flip when the bubbles across the surface have broken and are STAYING OPEN rather than filling back in, which is the moment the top has set enough to hold. That is usually two to three minutes. The first side is the face and should come off an even, unbroken tan-brown with no pale centre and no dark ring. The second side needs only forty seconds or so; it stays pale, and it is the inside. Cook them all, and as they come out of the pan, stack them and cover them with a cloth. Assemble while they are still warm. Pair pale side to pale side, put a generous mound of anko on one, doming it high in the centre and leaving a clear 1cm margin all round, and press the second one gently on top so the paste spreads to the edge without escaping. Then WRAP EACH ONE IN CLING FILM WHILE IT IS STILL WARM and leave it for at least half an hour. The trapped steam softens the crumb and marries the two halves, and it is the difference between two pancakes with jam in them and a dorayaki.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 1 hr 45 min (anko) + 15 min","kcal":"~420–480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 11g, Carbs 84g, Fat 5g, Sodium ~380mg","storage":"Wrapped individually in cling film, three days at room temperature and still soft on the third — that is the honey and the mirin doing their job, and it is why dorayaki became a shop cake rather than a home one. Do not refrigerate them: the fridge stales starch faster than a cupboard does and they go firm and dry. They freeze very well wrapped, for two months, and thaw at room temperature in about an hour. The anko keeps a week in the fridge in a sealed jar and freezes for three months; make far more than you need, because it is the base of half of Japanese confectionery and it is the part that takes the time.","chefNotes":"Boil the beans three minutes and throw the first water away. Sugar goes in only when a bean crushes to nothing between your fingers — early sugar keeps them hard for good. Stop the paste looser than you want it. Rest the batter thirty minutes. Wipe the pan almost dry. Pour, never spread. Flip when the bubbles stay open. Wrap them warm.","pairsWith":"Green tea, and specifically a plain sencha or a roasted hojicha rather than anything sweet or milky — the bitterness is doing structural work against the sugar.","trivia":"Sweet beans strike an unfamiliar palate as a strange idea, and the reason they exist is a genuinely interesting absence: Japan built its entire confectionery tradition with almost no dairy. No butter, no cream, no milk to speak of, for most of its history. In European sweets, fat is what carries sweetness and gives body — cream, butter, custard, ganache. Japanese wagashi had to solve the same problem with what was available, and the answer was azuki: a bean that cooks down to a smooth, starchy, faintly protein-rich paste that coats the mouth the way a fat does without being one. Sweet red bean is not an eccentric ingredient dropped into a Western dessert; it is a different and entirely coherent answer to the question of what makes a sweet thing satisfying. The beans carry weight beyond the kitchen too — red is a colour that wards off misfortune, which is why azuki turn up at births, weddings and New Year, cooked with rice into the celebratory sekihan. And there is a national split on texture that people hold real opinions about: tsubuan, left chunky with the skins in, against koshian, pushed through a sieve until it is completely smooth. Shops state which they use.","howThisFeels":"Peeling the cling film off one that has been sitting for an hour, and finding it has gone from two pancakes and a filling into a single soft thing.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover anko on hot buttered toast is a Nagoya breakfast institution and it is exactly as good as it sounds.","(easy) Stir a spoonful of anko into a mug of hot milk, or into plain yoghurt, for something between a pudding and a drink.","(creative) Any pancakes that came out badly shaped are still perfectly good: tear them into a bowl, warm a spoonful of anko with a splash of water to loosen it into a sauce, and pour it over with a scoop of vanilla ice cream."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Quick Anko from Tinned Beans (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":8,"howThisFeels":"Twenty minutes from deciding you want something sweet to actually eating it.","trivia":"Tinned red kidney beans are not azuki and the paste will be a shade earthier and less delicate, but the method collapses from two hours to fifteen minutes and the pancakes are unchanged. The one thing that cannot be skipped is the rinsing: tinned beans sit in a salty, starchy liquid that will taste of tin in a sweet paste, so rinse them under running water until the water runs completely clear. Since the beans are already fully soft, the sugar law does not apply here — there is nothing left to toughen — which is the single genuine advantage of the shortcut.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"80g dried azuki beans"}],"addIng":[{"item":"240g tinned red kidney or adzuki beans, drained"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Rinse the tinned beans thoroughly under cold running water until it runs completely clear, then drain them well. Put them in a pan with the sugar, the salt and 80ml of water and cook over a medium heat for twelve to fifteen minutes, mashing with the back of a wooden spoon as you go, until it is a thick paste that holds a line when you drag the spoon across the bottom of the pan. Stop it loose, cool it completely, and it is ready. Everything from the pancake batter onward is unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Classic Dorayaki","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"That first even, unblemished brown face lifting off the pan — and immediately wanting to make the rest better than that one.","trivia":"Cake flour matters here for the same reason it matters in tempura, from the other direction: lower protein means a softer, more tender crumb, and a dorayaki made with strong bread flour is noticeably chewy. If you only have plain flour, replace 10g of the 45g with cornflour. The bicarbonate of soda rather than baking powder is deliberate too — it needs an acid to react with, which the honey provides, and it also nudges the batter alkaline, which speeds browning and gives that even mahogany face that is the whole aesthetic of the thing.","delta":{}},{"name":"Matcha & Cream (Nama Dorayaki)","icon":"🍵","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"Cold cream against a warm pancake, and the small bitterness of the matcha arriving a second after the sweetness.","trivia":"Nama dorayaki is the modern bakery version — nama meaning fresh or raw, used in Japanese food names to mean something perishable that has to be eaten today. Whipped cream is layered with the anko rather than replacing it, and the two together do exactly what the tradition originally worked around: it is a dairy solution and a bean solution to the same problem, stacked. The matcha in the batter is not decorative either, since its tannic bitterness is the only thing standing between this version and being simply very sweet. Sift the matcha with the flour or it will refuse to disperse and leave green specks.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100ml cream, whipped to firm peaks"},{"item":"4g matcha powder"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Sift the matcha in with the cake flour and the bicarbonate — added to the wet mixture it clumps and refuses to disperse, leaving bitter green specks. Whip the cream to firm peaks, firmer than you would for a dessert, because a soft cream will squeeze straight out of the sides under the weight of the top pancake. Let the pancakes cool completely before assembling this version, which is the one exception to assembling warm; warm pancake melts cream. Spread anko on the bottom pancake, pipe or spoon the cream in a dome on top of it, and press the second pancake on gently. It cannot be wrapped and kept, and it needs eating within a couple of hours."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Chawanmushi","target":"japan-chawanmushi","emoji":"🥚"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Tempura","target":"japan-tempura","emoji":"🍤"}]}
];
